  <title>5.14.4: Using Function Notation to Compare Linear and Exponential Functions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01505</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.14.4: Using Function Notation to Compare Linear and Exponential Functions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>141e7e23-978d-48c1-93c1-bf979f7d5d14</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section id="sect-00001"><title>Cool Down Activity</title>
<para id="para-00001">The function \(f\) is given by \(f(x)=10x+3\), and the function \(g\) is given by \(g(x)=2^x\). For each question, show your reasoning.</para>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise id="exer-00001">
  <problem id="prob-00001">
    <para id="para-00002">Which function reaches 50 first?</para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00001">
  <para id="para-00003">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00004">\(f(5)=53\) and \(g(5)=32\), so \(f\) reaches 50 first.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise id="exer-00002">
  <problem id="prob-00002">
    <para id="para-00005">Which function reaches 100 first?</para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00002">
    
    
  
  <para id="para-00006">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00007">\(f(7)=73\) and \(g(7)=128\), so \(g\) reaches 100 first.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
</section>
</content>
